25  # Supabase Management API call sites this guard can still SEE.
    #
    # THIS IS A COVERAGE FLOOR, NOT AN OFFENDER CEILING, AND THE DIRECTION IS INVERTED
    # RELATIVE TO THIS REPO'S OTHER .highwater FILES. lint-diagnosis-claims.highwater and
    # lint-trap-tempfile-ownership's baseline count OFFENDERS, so they ratchet DOWN and their
    # header says "do not RAISE this to make a build pass". This one counts CALL SITES THE
    # EXTRACTOR STILL MATCHES, so:
    #
    #   DO NOT LOWER THIS NUMBER TO MAKE A BUILD PASS.
    #
    # A drop is the failure. It means either a call site was genuinely deleted (fine -- lower
    # this in the SAME commit as the deletion, and say which site) or the extractor went blind:
    # a pathspec typo, a renamed tree, a reworded `API=` assignment, a comment-marker change.
    # A guard that stops matching prints "0 violations" and looks exactly like a clean repo,
    # which is the class 924994b2f closed. This number is the only thing that tells them apart.
    #
    # Growth is legitimate. A new Management API caller RAISES it; the guard prints a note
    # asking you to. Raising is free, lowering needs a reason.
    #
    # WHAT THIS NUMBER COUNTS, EXACTLY. Non-comment lines, in the union of the two assemblies, that
    # match a Management API CALL CONSTRUCT: a host token (the literal `https://api.supabase.com`
    # or a `$VAR`/`${VAR}` used as the host) immediately followed by `/v1/projects/`. It is a
    # CENSUS OF THE SCANNED SCOPE, NOT OF THE REPO -- it counts only what the pathspec recorded
    # verbatim in the script header walks, and only what the call-construct anchor detects.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT counted, each verified by reading the line:
    #   - `/v1/projects/` with no host token (assertion strings in
    #     apply-inngest-rls-dev-workflow.test.sh:169-170, postgrest-reload-schema.test.sh:117)
    #   - comment lines (inngest.tf:218, 0002_dev_inngest_tables_lockdown.sql:60)
    #   - Hetzner's `/v1/servers/`, `/v1/actions/`, `/v1/images/` in scripts/cutover-inngest.sh.
    #     The anchor is `/v1/projects/` and not `/v1/` precisely so a Supabase host-pin rule
    #     cannot red three correct Hetzner lines.
    #   - apps/web-platform/infra/cron-egress-allowlist.txt:66, the bare hostname
    #     `api.supabase.com` with no scheme and no path -- in neither assembly by construction.
    #
    # PROVENANCE, 23 -> 25 (same PR, 2026-08-27). Measured, not estimated: the Phase 3
    # workstream landed `scripts/supabase-logs-query.sh` and `scripts/lib/supabase-logs-verdict.sh`
    # on this branch, adding two Management API call sites. That is legitimate GROWTH, and the
    # guard asked for the raise in a note. Recording the widening here rather than silently
    # editing the digit is the discipline lint-diagnosis-claims.highwater established: a number
    # that moves without a reason attached stops being evidence of anything.
    #
    # THE LEAD MUST RE-MEASURE BEFORE MERGE. This branch is collecting three workstreams. 25 is
    # the census as of this commit; any further caller added by integration raises it again.
    # Re-run `scripts/lint-supabase-deprecated-endpoints.sh --census` and set this to what it
    # prints. Leaving it low is not "safe" -- slack on a coverage floor is exactly the silent
    # slack this ratchet replaced.
    #
    # The three `advisors/security` sites inside this census are WAIVED, not absent. The waiver
    # lives in the script's DENY array with its date and justification; removing it is what
    # turns this population red, and that is the guard's live non-vacuity proof.
